pmc:NewThoughtShinnCrossedHerRedSea a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Restoration of lost money; crossing one's Red Sea"@en, "Rückerstattung verlorenen Geldes; Durchquerung des eigenen Roten Meeres"@de ;
    skos:definition "The suit was settled without trial, the manager repaying monthly; the relative gave all to the pianist, leaving her a large bank account; her goodwill parted the waters."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a capacity: a state the text holds attainable."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ScovelShinnSecretDoorToSuccess ;
    dcterms:source "Florence Scovel Shinn, The Secret Door to Success (1940), in The Writings of Florence Scovel Shinn, eBook ed. (www.Self-Improvement-eBooks.com, 2007), Book Three, ch. 7 \"Crossing Your Red Sea\", p. 192; read in the archive.org text layers TheCompleteWorksOfFlorenceScovelShinn and the-writing-of-florence-scovel-shinn of this eBook; agreement with the 1940 DeVorss print unchecked" .

pmc:NewThoughtShinnCrossedHerRedSeaPresupposesInitiationInForgiveness a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Forgiving before success returns"@en, "Vergebung, bevor der Erfolg zurückkehrt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author told the pianist her success would return when she was able to forgive the manager."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:NewThoughtShinnRecoveryPresupposesForgiveness (The Game of Life, p. 73): there forgiveness opens the universal bank for collecting a loss; here a client is told her success comes back once she forgives the man who took her funds."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:NewThoughtShinnCrossedHerRedSea ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:NewThoughtShinnInitiationInForgiveness ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought ;
