pmc:NewThoughtShinnDesiringSincerely a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sincere desire without anxiety catches up with the thing desired"@en, "Aufrichtiges Wünschen ohne Angst holt das Ersehnte ein"@de ;
    skos:definition "Desiring sincerely and without anxiety makes the desire crystallize outwardly; the righteous desire is an echo from the Infinite, already perfect as an idea in divine mind; inventors catch up with ideas (the telephone was seeking Bell)."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ScovelShinnSecretDoorToSuccess ;
    dcterms:source "Florence Scovel Shinn, The Secret Door to Success (1940), in The Writings of Florence Scovel Shinn, eBook ed. (www.Self-Improvement-eBooks.com, 2007), Book Three, ch. 12 \"Catch Up with Your Good\", p. 227; read in the archive.org text layers TheCompleteWorksOfFlorenceScovelShinn and the-writing-of-florence-scovel-shinn of this eBook; agreement with the 1940 DeVorss print unchecked" .

pmc:NewThoughtShinnSelfishDesires a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Selfish or harmful desires return to harm the sender"@en, "Selbstsüchtige oder schädliche Wünsche kehren zurück und schaden dem Absender"@de ;
    skos:definition "Desires that harm others always come back on whoever sent them."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:NewThoughtShinnKarmicDebt (The Game of Life, p. 41): there one case of a strong-willed desire that won a house and cost a husband; here a general rule that desires harming others recoil on the one who sends them."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:NewThoughtShinnRighteousDesire ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NewThought ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ScovelShinnSecretDoorToSuccess ;
